integrity via lipid peroxidation, causing ion leakage and wilting. These interconnected
mechanisms collectively stunt growth, lower yields, and threaten agricultural sustainability in heavy metal contaminated regions [11].
To mitigate contamination, sourcing organically grown Nigella products and verifying third-party lab certifications for heavy metals and pesticides are critical. While its
therapeutic potential is significant, safe consumption hinges on rigorous agricultural and
processing standards to balance benefits with contamination risks.
1.1 Heavy metals analysis in medicinal plants

The quantitative as well as qualitative analysis of heavy metals in medicinal plant species requires precise and reliable analytical techniques. Conventional analytical tools for
trace metals analysis include inductively coupled plasma mass spectrometry (ICP-MS)
and atomic absorption spectrometry (AAS), that offer high sensitivity and multi-element
detection. However, ICP-MS is cost-prohibitive for routine testing in resource-limited
settings, while traditional AAS methods often involve complex sample preparation and
lengthy protocols [12]. In contrast, FAAS has emerged as a robust, cost-effective alternative for quantifying heavy metals in medicinal plants. FAAS combines simplicity with
adequate sensitivity for regulatory compliance, particularly when analyzing common
contaminants like Pb and Cd, which have well-defined permissible limits in herbal products [3]. Recent advancements in FAAS, such as slurry sampling and microwave-assisted
