Abstract
Bioactive compounds containing the four most common and often used powdered spices for food
preparation have been studied concerning lead contamination and human health risk assessment,
especially in branded and nonbranded categories. A total of 72 spice samples were analysed using
Varian AA240FS flame atomic absorption spectrometry. A validated and optimized method using hot
plate digestion was used for this study. Freshly prepared standards were used to construct a calibration
curve. The overall range of lead levels in branded and nonbranded spice samples was found to be <
0.27–2.80, < 0.27–0.48, < 0.27–13.83, 0.28–8.19 and < 0.27–5.56, < 0.27–3.84, < 0.27–37.29, 0.29–0.65
for cumin powder, red pepper chili, turmeric powder, and coriander powder, respectively. The nonbranded
item has shown slightly higher value than branded items except coriander powder. Approximately 90% of
the spice samples were shown to be within the maximum allowable limit by the Bangladesh Standard.
The studied Pb level was inconsistent in both categories of spices but did not have any health
implications in the case of carcinogenic and noncarcinogenic studies. Overall, Pb concentrations in most
common spices collected from Dhaka city were found to be slightly higher in nonbranded items. This
shows us that it is of vital importance that the areas where spices are collected are clean, especially by
means of Pb, because these spices can cause more benefits than harm they may provide in such a
contamination.

Introduction
Natural bioactive compounds in different medicinal plants, herbs and spices have natural power in drug
development for thousands of years. From these natural sources, several drugs and bioactive
compounds have originated, and people are using them as ethno-medicines. In addition, it has a vital role
in the human diet to boost the flavour, colour and aroma of foods [1–3]. Recently, many computational
studies have found potential bioactives from these natural sources against the targets of SARS-CoV-2 [4].
Spices are biologically important drugs that are keen to taste and aroma and are also used to treat
various diseases [5]. Spice biochemicals are well known for their well-being role in a wide variety of
antioxidant, anti-inflammatory, and antimicrobial activities [4, 6]. For example, curcumin was the most
important bioactive compound released from turmeric and was exposed to an antiviral mode of action in
the case of many viruses, such as hepatitis and influenza viruses [7, 8]. Recently, scientists found that
spice bioactive piperine has shown a higher binding affinity with the SARS-CoV-2 spike protein receptorbinding domain and Mpro than few of the currently used drugs [9]. More recently, another study correlated
the incidence of COVID-19 and the consumption of spices in 163 countries. Interestingly, the countries
with lower intakes of spices per capita revealed a higher number of COVID-19 cases as well as mortality
per million populations. Furthermore, the retrieval rate was also higher in countries with a higher intake of
spices [10]. The beneficial effect of spice bioactivity might be due to their immunomodulatory and antiinflammatory potential observed against various diseases [11]. The severity of COVID-19 escalates
mostly because of the hyperinflammatory response mediated by cytokine storms, leading to acute
respiratory distress syndrome. Spice bioactive compounds are known to revoke proinflammatory
Page 2/21


cytokines and have the potential to reduce respiratory distress [4, 12]. In view of this biological
importance, people in the South Asian region use spices in cooking processes to impart aroma, colour
and taste of food and sometimes mask unwanted smells as well as ethno-medicine. It is also cherished
for its colouring as a preservative and fumigant [13].
In contrast, these notable biologically active compounds containing plant-like spices are being
contaminated through different outsiders, such as organic and inorganic pollutants that are also
bioactive adversely towards the human body. The decline in environmental quality occurs as a
consequence of countless diverse human activities. Undesirable changes in the environment cause
pollution for unsafe activities on plants, animals and humans. However, the cruelty of the environmental
impact and harmful effect on human health depend on the nature and concentration of the pollutants
[14]. An individual group of environmental contaminants of carbon-based and inorganic substances
maintains some explicit characteristics, such as harmfulness, persistence, and bioaccumulation, also
called persistent toxic pollutants, which remain in the environment for a long time with non or low
biodegradability in nature and are transferred to the food chain [15]. The most considerable category of
this is persistent pollutants, which are able to create severe impacts and threats at a global scale, not only
to the environmental components (water, air, soil) but also to human health [16]. If the inorganic
persistent chemicals are classified into different subcategories, heavy metals will be an extremely toxic
part of that category, which is nonbiodegradable. Heavy metals can easily accumulate in soils, water and
biological sections. The accumulated or transferred heavy metals enter the food chain, finally disturbing
the usual functions of the human body [17]. Studies show that a major route of exposure to heavy metals
is the consumption of contaminated food; more than 420,000 people pass away each year after eating
contaminated foodstuff worldwide, and one out of ten individuals becomes affected [18]. Consumption
of contaminated food can cause more than 200 risks of acute and chronic diseases. It has been found to
increase the risk of cardiovascular, neurological, and renal diseases and digestive infections to cancer
[19]. For example, cancer is one of the prominent causes of death and is projected to reach 28.4 million
cases of cancer worldwide in 2040, which is a 47% upsurge from 2020. According to the International
Agency for Research on Cancer, in Bangladesh, the cancer-related mortality rate was 7.5% and will reach
up to 13% by 2030, which is almost twice the rate of 2005[20].
Several studies have shown that carcinogenic lead (Pb) in the human body comes mainly from food
items, and approximately 50% originates from plants [21]. Pb is one of the most common heavy metal
contaminants in the total food chain. Plants are remarkably unsafe to it. Pb has no biological importance
in plant life, even though it can generate morphological, physiological, and biochemical problems [22].
However, Pb can easily accumulate in the soil and roots. Adsorption of lead in the roots has been
detected in several kinds of plants. Captivated lead is stored in the roots, and some fraction of it is
conveyed to aerial plant parts. Then, come leafy greens such as lettuce and Swiss chard. Lead in plants
also has an effect on soil enzymatic actions and microbial biomass at low concentrations and an
inhibitory effect at high concentrations. The Pb contents with increased values linearly decreased enzyme
actions, which was obvious, particularly for aryl sulfatase. Most importantly, Pb can survive up to 2000
years on Earth and is harmful to plants in several ways [22, 23].
Page 3/21


Bangladesh is a densely populated country, with approximately 165 million people consuming a vast
amount of spices every day. The spice market is developing to branded with packaged form since users
are screening unwillingness to go for traditional systems of processing spices. As the females of today
are full of activity with their careers, the choice of branded spice powder is better day by day [24]. The
transformation of nonbranded to branded powdered spices is developing by approximately 15% a year
[25]. In a recent study, 42% of users chose only branded spice powder, and 58% chose nonbranded spice
powder [26]. People’s increasing consciousness about hygienic spices and busy urban life has led to a
rise in branded spice consumption. From the survey, it was found that on average, 90.70% of people
believed that the spice powders were not of good quality. A study on the types of adulterants added to the
spice powder according to consumers’ suspects revealed that the addition of high-grade low-grade raw
spice was at the top position, followed by brick dust and sand. Based on consumers’ opinion, cancer,
chronic nephritis and high blood pressure were the top possible diseases that may be associated with the
consumption of contaminated spice powder [27]. Since consumers have many options for choosing
